<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narasimhavarman I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Mamalla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narasimhavarman I, known as Mamalla, 'great wrestler', was king of the Pallava dynasty from about 630 to 668. He defeated the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> and in 642, with his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paranjothi">Paranjothi</a>, captured the Chalukya capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a>, taking the title Vatapikonda. He sent naval expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> to support the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manavanna">Manavanna</a>, and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a> is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i.jpg" alt="Narasimhavarman I" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G41rn8 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamallapuram_si0497.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Narasimhavarman I was the son of the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a>, whose love of art he shared. The Chalukya emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> had raided the northern Pallava provinces and forts in his father's time without taking the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, and the long conflict between the two dynasties passed to the son. The Sri Lankan chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Culavamsa</a>, calls him Kanduvethi, an epithet that historians link to the name Kadavan, later borne by the Kadava chiefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> attacked again, Narasimhavarman defeated him in several battles, among them one at Manimangalam, 20 miles east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, where the Pallava king boasted that he saw the back of his enemy as he tore his army apart. He then advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a>, where Pulakeshin II was defeated and killed, and the city was never again a capital. Many grants compare the destruction of Vatapi to the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agastya">Agastya</a>'s killing of a demon of that name. His general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paranjothi">Paranjothi</a>, honoured as one of the 63 Nayanar saints, is said to have destroyed the city himself.</p><p>The Sinhalese prince Manavarman lived at his court and helped him against Pulakeshin. In return Narasimhavarman twice sent an army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> for him, and the second expedition succeeded; the Kasakudi copper plates refer to the conquest of Sri Lanka. In 640 the Chinese traveller Hiuen Tsang visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, and the Nayanar poet-saints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a> lived in his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Narasimhavarman completed the works his father had begun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a>, and the Pancha Rathas, a complex of monolithic rock-cut temples, were made in his reign; most of the monuments of Mahabalipuram, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, were finished under him. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-ii">Mahendravarman II</a> succeeded him in 668. In modern Tamil literature Kalki Krishnamurthy based Sivagamiyin Sabadham on his early years and Parthiban Kanavu on the later years of his rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narasimhavarman I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 642 the Pallava king <b>Narasimhavarman I</b> defeated him, occupied Vatapi and took the title Vatapikonda, ‘conqueror of Vatapi’; Pulakeshin II apparently died in the fighting.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahendravarman's son <b>Narasimhavarman I</b>, known as Mamalla, turned the tide: he defeated Pulakeshin in several battles and in 642, with his general Paranjoti, captured and sacked the Chalukya capital Vatapi, where Pulakeshin died, earning the title Vatapikonda, conqueror of Vatapi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
